steps.text:
`props.items.map(...)` will throw **TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')** when `props.items` is **`undefined` or `null`** at the time the component renders.  
In React you usually see this when the parent component hasn’t supplied the data yet (e.g., it’s still loading) or when the prop wasn’t given a default value.

Below are the most common ways to guard against that error and make your component robust.

---

## 1. Give the prop a sane default (default Props or default argument)

### a) Using `defaultProps` (class components or function components that still use it)

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// If the parent forgets to pass `items`, it will default to an empty array.
ItemList.defaultProps = {
  items: [],          // ← default value
};
```

### b) Using a default value in the function signature (modern functional components)

```jsx
function ItemList({ items = [] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

Both approaches guarantee that `items` is always an array, so `map` will never be called on `undefined`.

---

## 2. Guard the rendering with a conditional check

If you prefer to show a different UI while the data is loading, check for the existence of the array before you map it:

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  if (!items) {
    // Data is still loading or missing – render a placeholder.
    return <p>Loading items…</p>;
  }

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

You can also combine the guard with a fallback UI:

```jsx
return (
  <ul>
    {(items || []).map(item => (
      <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
);
```

---

## 3. Use optional‑chaining (ES2020) for a concise one‑liner

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items?.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      )) ?? <p>No items available.</p>}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

`items?.map` only calls `map` when `items` is not `null`/`undefined`. If it *is* undefined, the expression evaluates to `undefined`, and you can render a fallback.

---

## 4. Validate the prop type (helps catch bugs early)

```jsx
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function ItemList({ items }) {
  // …same rendering code
}

ItemList.propTypes = {
  // Guarantees that `items` must be an array (or undefined, if you allow it)
  items: PropTypes.arrayOf(
    PropTypes.shape({
      id: PropTypes.oneOfType([PropTypes.string, PropTypes.number])
        .isRequired,
      name: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
    })
  ),
};
```

If a parent passes something other than an array, React will warn you in the console (in development mode).

---

## 5. Common scenario: data coming from an async request

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState(null); // start as null

  React.useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/items')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setItems(data))
      .catch(err => console.error(err));
  }, []);

  // While loading, `items` is null → ItemList receives null.
  return <ItemList items={items} />;
}
```

Because `items` starts as `null`, the `ItemList` component should handle that case (see the conditional guard or default value). Once the fetch resolves, `items` becomes an array and the list renders normally.

---

## 6. TL;DR – Minimal fix you can paste in

If you just want the quickest change:

```jsx
function ItemList({ items = [] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

or, if you need a loading placeholder:

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items?.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      )) ?? <p>Loading items…</p>}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

---

### Recap of the steps

| What to do | Why it works |
|------------|--------------|
| **Give a default (`items = []` or `defaultProps`)** | Guarantees `items` is always an array. |
| **Guard with `if (!items) …`** | Shows a loading / empty state until data arrives. |
| **Optional chaining (`items?.map`)** | Calls `map` only when `items` exists. |
| **PropTypes / TypeScript** | Catches incorrect prop types early. |
| **Initialize state as `null`/`undefined` and handle it** | Matches the typical async‑fetch pattern. |

Pick the pattern that best fits your UI/UX requirements, and the `TypeError` will disappear. Happy coding!
